    "question": "Chad bought 6 packages of cat food and 2 packages of dog food. Each package of cat food contained 9 cans, and each package of dog food contained 3 cans. How many more cans of cat food than dog food did Chad buy?",
    "gold": "48",
    "answer": "Chad's cat food total was 6 packages * 9 cans = <<6*9=54>>54 cans of cat food.\nChad's dog food total was 2 packages * 3 cans = <<2*3=6>>6 cans of dog food.\nChad had 54 cans of cat food - 6 cans of dog food = <<54-6=48>>48 more cans of cat food.\n#### 48"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many more cans of cat food than dog food Chad bought, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of cans of cat food Chad bought.\n2. Calculate the total number of cans of dog food Chad bought.\n3. Find the difference between the number of cans of cat food and the number of cans of dog food.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of cans of cat food:\nChad bought 6 packages of cat food, and each package contains 9 cans.\n\\[\n6 \\text{ packages} \\times 9 \\text{ cans per package} = 54 \\text{ cans of cat food}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the total number of cans of dog food:\nChad bought 2 packages of dog food, and each package contains 3 cans.\n\\[\n2 \\text{ packages} \\times 3 \\text{ cans per package} = 6 \\text{ cans of dog food}\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the difference between the number of cans of cat food and the number of cans of dog food:\n\\[\n54 \\text{ cans of cat food} - 6 \\text{ cans of dog food} = 48 \\text{ more cans of cat food}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Chad bought \\(\\boxed{48}\\) more cans of cat food than dog food.",
